.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-trace - Everything about one payment: transactions, sync errors, deliveries, disputes, operator actions


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin trace  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Answer "what happened to this payment" in one command, whoever the payment belongs to.

.PP
The payment is read with its transactions, and then everything else that names it:
the sync errors (indexer callbacks about it that could not be applied), the webhook
deliveries across every subscription, its disputes, and what operators did to it.
\&'totals' says how many of each exist, in case one kind has more than a page.

.PP
--timeline prints the same facts as one chronological list instead: the payment's own
history with the sync errors, deliveries, disputes and operator actions in their place.

.PP
The argument is the payment's id (UUID) or its rail0_id (0x…, 64 hex).


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for trace

.PP
\fB--timeline\fP[=false]
	Print one chronological list instead of the sections


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin trace 0xrail0Id…
  rail0 admin trace 019e748b-… --timeline --pretty
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin(1)\fP
